<AashaCare />
<div class="seo-answer"><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Child and adolescent ADHD and autism assessments are coordinated through Aasha Early Intervention Centre; adult assessment enquiries are handled by Mind Grace. A clinician considers history, functioning and relevant information from different settings; no single checklist provides a diagnosis.</div><section class="seo-hero"><p class="seo-kicker">Neurodevelopmental assessment</p><h1>ADHD and autism assessment in Greater Noida</h1><p class="seo-lead">Assessment considers development, communication, attention, behaviour, learning, sensory experiences, health, and everyday functioning across settings.</p><div class="seo-actions"><a class="btn" href="/aasha/book">Request a child assessment enquiry</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="/book?service=assessment">Ask about an adult assessment</a></div></section><section class="seo-section"><h2>Which doctor should I consult for an ADHD diagnosis?</h2><p>Start with the person’s age and the question you want answered. For a child or adolescent, contact <a href="/aasha">Aasha Early Intervention Centre</a>; its team can explain the relevant child assessment or consultation pathway. For an adult, contact Mind Grace through the <a href="/assessments">adult assessment information</a> or <a href="/book">appointment request page</a>. You do not need to choose a diagnostic label or arrive with a completed checklist.</p><p>Assessment responsibilities depend on the clinician, professional training, assessment question and the available information. A psychiatric consultation may consider medical history and other possible contributors, while psychological assessment may use structured interviews, rating scales, testing or observations when appropriate. The clinic can explain who will take the history, who will interpret tools, who will discuss findings and whether another professional should be involved. Ask these questions before confirming an assessment.</p><h2>What an assessment may include</h2><div class="seo-grid"><article class="seo-card"><h3>History and observation</h3><p>Developmental history, family observations, school feedback, and the person’s own experience may be gathered.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Structured tools</h3><p>Questionnaires or tests may be used when clinically appropriate; no single checklist gives a diagnosis.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Recommendations</h3><p>The outcome may include practical strategies, school guidance, follow-up, or referral.</p></article></div><h2>Child and adolescent assessment pathway</h2><p>For a child, the team may ask about early development, communication, attention, play, behaviour, learning, sleep, health and daily routines. Parents or caregivers may be asked for examples from home. School observations or prior reports can provide another perspective when available and when sharing them is appropriate. The assessment plan is chosen for the question being asked; it may involve more than one conversation or tool, and the number of visits should be confirmed with the clinic.</p><p>Aasha’s <a href="/aasha/developmental-assessment">developmental assessment page</a> describes its child pathway and the roles of its assessment team. A parent may also use the <a href="/aasha/developmental-check-in">developmental check-in</a> to organise everyday observations; it is a discussion aid, not a validated screening test or diagnostic instrument. A child can be referred for support even when a family does not yet have a clear label.</p><h2>Adult assessment pathway</h2><p>For adults, an assessment may consider current concerns alongside patterns over time, childhood history where relevant, work or study demands, relationships, sleep, physical health, medicines and other factors that can affect concentration or behaviour. The clinician may ask how the person functions across settings and what strategies have already helped. If a family member or older record provides useful context, the clinician can explain how to include it while respecting the adult’s privacy and consent.</p><p>ADHD and autism can overlap with other concerns, and a person can have more than one condition or none of the conditions initially suspected. A careful evaluation explores alternatives and co-occurring needs rather than relying on an online quiz, one observation or a single score. The assessment conclusion and any further referral are discussed with the person.</p><h2>What to prepare and ask</h2><p>Before the visit, write down the main questions, examples of situations that are difficult, strengths and strategies that help. Parents may bring developmental history, school feedback and previous reports if available. Adults may note when the pattern began, what it affects and any earlier evaluations. There is no need to collect every document before contacting the clinic.</p><ul><li>Who will conduct each part of the assessment and discuss the findings?</li><li>Which question is the assessment designed to answer?</li><li>How many appointments may be needed, and what could change that estimate?</li><li>Will there be a written report, feedback session, or recommendations, and are those included in the quoted fee?</li><li>How should school or family information be shared, and what consent is needed?</li></ul><p>Review <a href="/fees">assessment fees</a> and ask the clinic to confirm the exact scope, report arrangements and any separate feedback appointment before booking. The assessment fee can vary with the selected tools and complexity. Do not assume a report or number of sessions is included unless the clinic confirms it.</p><h2>Where to book and what happens next</h2><p>For children and adolescents, use <a href="/aasha/book">Aasha’s appointment request page</a> or call the coordination number published on the Aasha page. For adults, use the <a href="/book">Mind Grace request form</a> or contact the clinic. A request begins coordination; it does not confirm a slot or establish a diagnosis. The team can explain availability, the likely first appointment and which records may be useful. Do not put detailed clinical histories in a routine booking form.</p><p>Mind Grace and Aasha are not emergency services. If immediate safety is at risk, contact emergency services or go to the nearest emergency department. For other practical preparation, see the <a href="/process">first-visit guide</a>, <a href="/aasha">Aasha services</a> and <a href="/contact">clinic contact details</a>.</p><div class="seo-note"><strong>Clinical boundary:</strong> Online information cannot diagnose ADHD or autism. Assessment scope, duration, clinician roles and report format must be confirmed for each individual before booking.</div></section>
